    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Système de cote ÉnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="1" build="19">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.1b19</English>
                <French>v15.1b19</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="11">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.11</English>
                <French>v1.11</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Following Areas are showing signs of air leakage:



Right Door - bottom or door is leaky



Old basement windows that don't have the extra fixed pane are very leaky.



Old Chimney access through wall is letting air in at a high rate



Furnace rough ins through the exterior wall is leaky



Back windows on main floor are leaking around frames (corners)



Leakage around the back door



Large front window in living room is leaky around edges



Upstairs windows in master bedroom and small bedroom between the bath and other room are leaky around edges as well



Attic Hatch is leaky



</airSeal>
                <ceilings>The ceiling insulation is probably due for an upgrade. You could add to the insualtion that is there, or could rip the old R-7 batts and cellulose out and replace with new insulation. You would want to get around R-50 in the attic. That would be adding another R-20 on top of the existing insulation. </ceilings>
                <foundation>The remaining part of the exterior that hasnt been insulated around the foundation should be completed.</foundation>
                <windows>All of the windows in the basement should be replaced. The front 2 and one on the right that have the extra fixed pane and aren't showing much air leakage should still be replaced as the windows outside of these would be leaky and there would still be quite a bit of conductive heat loss through these.



The windows in the rest of the house should be upgraded to windows rated for Energy Star Zone 3. This would be expensive to replace all the windows. If there is a way to seal up the air leaks around the windows than it would probably be cheaper and would help with heat loss.</windows>
                <doors>The doors could all use new weather stripping and door sweeps on all of them to reduce the amount of air leakage and heat loss.</doors>
                <HeatingSystem>Condensing Propane Furnace:



You can use existing ductwork. You will  not need a chimney as this unit uses PVC pipe as an exhasut becuase the temperature of the exhaust is so low. The combustion air will be sealed off from the interior of the home and can avoid back drafting. 



You don't have to worry about corrosion or soot build up like you would with a condensing oil furnace (though this should not happen) as propane is easier to extract heat from than oil.



These units are 95% efficient.</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>You have a perfectly south facing roof. Possibly putting a solar pv array on the south and either east or west sides could be an option. Talk to a solar company about this possibility.</Renewables>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
